# Generated, vendored or data-shaped - never format these. Every entry is bytes
# a tool owns or bytes whose exact text is the value: a formatter that rewrites
# either turns a current artefact into a stale one, and `Scripts/Feed.mjs`
# compares the bundler's output byte for byte.

# Build output. Both entries are anchored on purpose - a pattern with a slash in
# it is relative to this file, while a bare `Target/` matches that directory name
# at any depth, which is how a real source directory gets hidden from the
# formatter for as long as the rule stays loose.
/Target/
rust/Target/

# Unanchored on purpose: every package and the workspace root can hold one.
node_modules/

# The feed tools' output. `bundle.mjs` writes `batch-NN.txt`, `tree.txt` and
# `manifest.json`; the batches are the payload a chat composer receives, so
# rewrapping a line would change what is sent.
.hermes/bundle/
.hermes/research-bundle/

# Scratch: probes and throwaway trees. Never part of the repository, and a
# format pass over a fixture would rewrite the bytes a test asserts on.
.hermes/tmp/

# The research captures are evidence, not documentation. `docs/Research.md` says
# they are never edited, and a capture's bytes are what it recorded - rewrapping
# a paragraph to fit a column would edit the record. `catalog.mjs` beside them is
# source and stays formattable.
.hermes/research/*.md

# Generated from `scenarios/generate.mjs`. The generator owns these bytes and
# `scenarios/run.mjs` compares them exactly, so a format pass that rewrites them
# turns a current artefact into a stale one - `JSON.stringify(document, null, "\t")`
# puts every array element on its own line and prettier keeps a short array on one.
scenarios/generated/

# Dependencies and locks. `Cargo.lock` is a resolution artefact and the tree
# treats it as local-only; the pnpm lockfiles are pnpm's to write.
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json
Cargo.lock
